# ironclaw-1.4.0-allow-loopback-egress.patch
#
# Target : nearai/ironclaw tag ironclaw-v1.4.0 (4cb47cfaf3fbacd3a2f575a8da654a6542d5fdfe) — the
#          exact source the Homebrew formula builds (brew info: versions.stable 1.4.0, url
#          .../archive/refs/tags/ironclaw-v1.4.0.tar.gz).
# Why    : Jyotish-OS's MCP server is bound to https://127.0.0.1:7791 by contract
#          (docs/CONTRACT.md "Ports (loopback only)"). IronClaw 1.4.0 refuses that in two
#          places, both by design (SSRF/private-network guard):
#            1. crates/substrates/ironclaw_network — the single enforcement point of
#               `NetworkPolicy.deny_private_ip_ranges` for host-mediated egress (hosted-MCP
#               tool calls + discovery, builtin.http). Every policy producer hard-codes it to
#               true for networked capabilities (capability_surface.rs:225,
#               hosted_mcp_manifest.rs:180, mcp.rs:196, builtin_capability_policy.rs:536,
#               product_capability.rs:251, ironclaw_auth http.rs:137).
#            2. crates/extensions/ironclaw_extension_host/src/hosted_mcp_admission.rs — the
#               endpoint admission for `builtin.extension_register_hosted_mcp` rejects
#               `localhost` and every IP literal outright. This is the ONLY route by which
#               a user-supplied hosted MCP server becomes callable in 1.4.0: a package
#               installed from the filesystem catalog (`ironclaw extension install`) is
#               ManifestSource::InstalledLocal, which `hosted_http_mcp_url`
#               (ironclaw_extension_registry/src/hosted_mcp_discovery.rs:83) never treats as
#               a hosted-MCP provider, so its pinned tools keep an empty allow-list
#               (v3.rs:621) and fail the ApplyNetworkPolicy obligation with `network_denied`
#               before any socket is opened — for public and private endpoints alike.
# Change : one opt-in environment variable, IRONCLAW_EGRESS_ALLOW_LOOPBACK=1 (also
#          true/yes/on), read once per process (ironclaw_network::loopback_egress_allowed).
#          When set: (a) an IP in 127.0.0.0/8 or exactly ::1 is no longer a denied private
#          target for deny_private_ip_ranges; (b) hosted-MCP admission accepts an endpoint
#          whose host is `localhost`, 127.0.0.0/8 or ::1. 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16,
#          169.254/16, 100.64/10, 0/8, fc00::/7, fe80::/10, documentation/multicast ranges,
#          IPv4-mapped IPv6 forms (incl. ::ffff:127.0.0.1) and every other IP literal stay
#          denied exactly as before, in both places. Unset (or any other value): behaviour
#          byte-identical to upstream. The allow-list match, https-only scheme, port match,
#          egress-byte caps and TLS trust (SSL_CERT_FILE) are untouched. A one-time
#          tracing::warn! announces the exemption at first policy evaluation.
# Tests  : ironclaw_network policy.rs `loopback_exemption_tests` (flag parsing off-by-default
#          and strict; everything non-public denied without the flag; only loopback exempt
#          with it) and ironclaw_extension_host hosted_mcp_admission.rs
#          `loopback_opt_in_admits_only_loopback_endpoints` (loopback admitted only with the
#          flag; other private/public literals and http:// rejected either way). Upstream's
#          `canonical_endpoint_rejects_credential_and_private_literal_forms` still passes.
# Apply  : git -C vendor/ironclaw-fork apply ../../patches/ironclaw-1.4.0-allow-loopback-egress.patch
# Build  : cargo build --release -p ironclaw   (package `ironclaw`, dir crates/app/ironclaw_cli)
# Revert : rm ~/.local/bin/ironclaw-jyotish — the Homebrew binary is never modified.
#
